__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store

# runs/ and registry/ are evidence, not build output — they are committed on purpose.

# Claude Code local state (agent worktrees, etc.) — not part of the repo.
.claude/

# --- what stays out of this repository, and on WHOSE say-so ---------------------------
#
# gnostrich/common-ground is PUBLIC (checked 2026-08-05, GitHub API: "private": false).
# Anything committed here is published.
#
# ROHAN'S INSTRUCTION, in his words:
#   "NEVER commit them or the corpus" (the Aristotle API keys and the Lean corpus)
#   "Do NOT commit the user's proprietary Lean corpus into the repo without first
#    confirming repo visibility with Rohan"
# Visibility is now confirmed: the repo is public, so the corpus stays out. That much is
# his rule and it is quoted rather than paraphrased.
#
# CLAUDE'S ENGINEERING JUDGEMENT, which is NOT his rule and is labelled as mine:
#   pool.jsonl and corpus.snapshot are derived from that corpus and carry its nu-strings
#   verbatim, so publishing them publishes the corpus by another route. They are also pure
#   functions of (corpus, seed, code), so keeping them out costs rebuild time and no
#   information. If Rohan wants them committed, that is his call to make and it overrides
#   this line.
#
# An earlier version of this file stated the above as a numbered "hard constraint" and a
# keepalive prompt I had written myself repeated it back as though it came from Rohan. It
# did not. Treating my own inference as his instruction is how a project acquires rules
# nobody agreed to, and it blocked the journal-durability fix for hours.
#
# corpus.local.json is the ONE file that names where a corpus lives — repo roots, export
# paths, excluded conversation ids. It is what makes this mechanism forkable: the template
# `seed/CORPUS.example.json` is committed, the real pointer is not, so anyone can take the
# engine without taking the shape of somebody's disk. tests/test_corpus_sources.py asserts
# this line still holds, because the seam is only real while the pointer stays out.
corpus.local.json
runs/pool.jsonl
runs/corpus.snapshot
# ANY SUFFIX. A backup copy is corpus bytes under a longer name, and `git add -A`
# swept `runs/corpus.snapshot.pre-demotion` into the index one commit after the
# control against exactly that was written. The control caught it; the pattern is
# what stops it happening again.
runs/corpus.snapshot*
runs/proposer.journal.jsonl
runs/proposer.control.json
runs/proposer.status.json

# seed_runs/ is the DEPLOY staging copy of exactly the three files above. It is ignored for
# the same reason they are: it holds the corpus's nu-strings, and this repo is public.
# `railway up` uploads the working TREE, not the git tree, so deploying does not need these
# tracked — and a `git add -A` that swept them in is how they reached a public branch once.
seed_runs/

# runs/BUILD is the SERVED-commit stamp and must never be committed: written before the
# commit that contains it, it would name the parent — a one-commit lag reported with the
# confidence of an exact answer, which is worse than the `unknown` it replaced. The deploy
# writes it (or sets CG_COMMIT) at push time, outside git.
runs/BUILD
runs/acceptance/
runs/glossary.jsonl
runs/gloss_validation.json
runs/gloss_ab.log
runs/audit.json

# ENV FILES, ANYWHERE. The operator's keys live in a scratchpad OUTSIDE this repository, and
# that location is the real protection. This is belt and braces for the moment somebody copies
# one in to debug something and `git add -A` publishes it — which is not hypothetical here:
# `runs/corpus.snapshot.pre-demotion` reached the index by exactly that route, one commit
# after the control against it was written. tests/test_key_exposure.py asserts this line.
*.env

# runs/fixture-D/ — the two-tier fixture's RAW artifacts. Ignored not because they are noise
# but because they carry CORPUS TEXT: every artifact holds the answer verbatim and the
# convicted sentences, which are the operator's private material quoted back. This repository
# is public. The fixture RECORD in seed/ carries the numbers, which is what a fixture is for;
# the claim text stays on the machine that measured it.
runs/fixture-*/

# The live-fire artifacts ARE committed — deliberately, and against the rule directly above
# them. `runs/fixture-*/` is ignored because it carries the answer verbatim; a livefire
# artifact carries the BRACKET SKELETON and nothing else, redacted in `Finding.__post_init__`
# and in `redact()` before it is ever written, with a control asserting both. They are the
# evidence a deploy LANDED, git is the only durable store this container has, and evidence
# that does not survive a reclaim is not evidence.
!runs/livefire/

# Agent scratch. The standing auditor writes its own retry runs while it works; the artifact
# that counts is the one `runs/livefire/` holds, written by the battery itself.
runs/livefire_retry/
